Among green coffees in the Purveyors catalog that publish a cup score, the score explains about half a percent of the variation in retail price. Country of origin explains 43%. The specialty market's main quality measure barely moves what buyers pay, and buyers have good reasons to discount it. Scores are not calibrated across sellers, the descriptions around them are written to sell, and some of the most valuable claims in coffee turn out to be false.

Ask a roaster what makes a particular coffee great and the answer will be some mix of origin, farm, variety, process, score, and a feeling about the importer. Ask how they know those claims are true, or whether this farm's last three harvests were any good, and the honest answer is usually that they don't. Coffee has plenty of descriptive data. What it lacks is a trustworthy, durable record of quality that a buyer can use before the bag is roasted.

## The signals don't travel

Cup scores are the clearest example. For Ethiopian coffees in the Purveyors catalog, average scores by supplier range from 86.4 to 91.0. The supplier with the highest scores also has the lowest median price.

