A score is soft judgment that has been turned into a number, and the number only means something if everyone uses the same scale. In finance, Liberti and Petersen put the test plainly: "Your interpretation of a 3 must be the same as mine" ([NBER](https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w25075/w25075.pdf)). Coffee fails that test. Twenty-six of the 37 retail suppliers we track publish no score at all, and the vocabulary around scores is not much better. Suppliers' own tasting notes name a fruit on 78% of washed listings and 85% of naturals, so "fruity" separates almost nothing.

The harder problem is that many claims cannot be checked at all. Origin, variety, and farming practice are credence attributes: a roaster cannot taste them. A Kona labeling lawsuit alleged that about 2.7 million pounds of Kona is grown each year while more than 20 million pounds is sold under the name ([Daily Coffee News](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2023/09/27/federal-judge-approves-final-12-million-settlement-in-kona-labeling-case/)). DNA testing of 88 samples labeled Gesha found that only 39% were genetically Gesha, mostly through nursery confusion rather than fraud ([Daily Coffee News](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2020/04/08/genetic-testing-reveals-the-true-nature-of-coffee-varieties/)). Story also changes perception directly: people served two identical coffees preferred the one falsely labeled eco-friendly, and paid more for it ([PLOS ONE](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0080719)).

Faced with signals that are inconsistent and partly untrue, buyers do the rational thing and mostly ignore them.

