## Buffett trusted records he could check

Warren Buffett is often summarized as an investor who looked at the whole business instead of the ticker. The business he looked at was a long, verifiable record. Berkshire's acquisition criteria asked for "demonstrated consistent earning power," adding that "future projections are of little interest to us" ([1987 letter](https://www.berkshirehathaway.com/letters/1987.html)). See's Candies, bought for $25 million, went on to produce $1.35 billion of pre-tax earnings on $32 million of reinvestment, which Buffett credited to an advantage "built by the See's family over a 50-year period" ([2007 letter](https://www.berkshirehathaway.com/letters/2007ltr.pdf)).

Later research found that Berkshire's outperformance becomes statistically insignificant once you control for exposure to cheap, safe, high-quality stocks ([Frazzini, Kabiller and Pedersen](https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w19681/w19681.pdf)). The qualities Buffett judged were real, and they showed up in audited numbers kept consistently for decades. Context becomes usable when it is recorded the same way over time and someone can check it.

## Wine made reputation usable

Wine is the obvious comparison, and its advantage is often described as romance. The more useful advantage is that wine found a legitimate way to store reputation so buyers can act on it. That system came out of fraud. After phylloxera destroyed more than 40% of French vines in the late nineteenth century, the shortage was filled with raisin wine and fakes. Growers rioted, and France spent about 30 years building origin law, ending with the appellation system in 1935. Those rules guarantee where the grapes grew and how the wine was made. A place name on a label therefore predicts something about what is in the glass, and every vintage is filed under the same verified name.

Prices then accumulated against those identities. Combris, Lecocq and Visser found that Bordeaux prices follow what is printed on the label, while blind jury grades follow how the wine tastes ([Economic Journal](https://ideas.repec.org/a/ecj/econjl/v107y1997i441p390-402.html)). A meta-analysis of more than 180 wine price models concludes that reputation matters more than current sensory quality, with one qualifier: producers have to sustain quality over time to earn it ([Oczkowski and Doucouliagos](https://ideas.repec.org/a/oup/ajagec/v97y2015i1p103-121..html)). Reputation in wine is the stored history of quality, and buyers trust it because the identity under it is enforced.

Coffee has moved in the opposite direction. One importer in our catalog lists nine lots under the identical name of a single Ethiopian farm and grade, with tasting notes ranging from toffee and cooked apple to hibiscus and tropical fruit. Another single estate appears across four suppliers in at least six processing styles. Processing experiments have made the farm name a weaker predictor of the cup.

## Coffee pays for identity, then forgets it

Six in ten stocked listings in our catalog carry a substantial farm narrative. Fewer than one in five carry any structured producer identity, and of roughly 400 farm names we have seen, only 17 link across more than one supplier. When a lot sells out, its listing disappears and its story goes with it. Early evidence suggests identity is what buyers value: within the same supplier and origin, listings that name a farm or producer price higher, while longer farm stories show no measurable effect. The sample with structured identity is still small, so that result is directional.

Ethiopia ran the larger experiment. When its commodity exchange began trading coffee without lot identity in 2008, the price gap between traceable and untraceable exports widened by 12 percentage points, an estimated $280 million or 26% of farmers' coffee income ([Leung, discussion paper](https://cri-world.com/publications/qed_dp_249.pdf)).

## The one long record coffee has

Cup of Excellence is the closest thing coffee has to a vintage record: national juries score lots blind and publish the winners. I compiled 4,927 winning lots from Brazil, Colombia, Costa Rica, El Salvador, Guatemala, Honduras and Nicaragua between 1999 and 2026 and linked farms across years.

Quality persists, weakly. About a quarter of first-time placers place again within five years, and about a third eventually do. Competition-adjusted scores correlate around 0.2 between a farm's appearances, against zero for randomly paired farms, and a returning top-ten farm finishes top ten again 45% of the time compared with 32% for other returners. A few estates build real records; El Injerto placed in 19 of 23 Guatemalan competitions. The market stores little of that history.

<BlogMetricChart
	title="At auction, buyers pay for this year's cup"
